# Words set to a rhythm: one note a syllable, the stressed syllable on the strong beat, a rhythm
# matched to a word, a line tapped as spoken, its length in beats, and a line of the child's own
# sung.
lesson music-words-to-rhythm v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=1 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Words set to a rhythm"
  goal "Set words to music with one note a syllable and the strong syllables on the strong beats."

  look {
    scene 24x10 {
      staff bar notes=[0.5, 0.5, 1, 0.5, 0.5, 1] beats=4 at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      When words are sung, each syllable gets its own note. Spoken words have a rhythm already: in
      butterfly, the first syllable is strong and long, and the next two are quick. A good setting
      keeps that rhythm and puts the strongest syllables on the strong beats, beat 1 and beat 3 in a
      bar of four, so the words sound as they do when we say them. The bar here is butterfly,
      butterfly: two quick notes and a longer one, twice.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.words-syllables k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show music.words-stress k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    level easy {
      worked music.words-beats n=6 q=0 level=medium
    }
    show music.words-fit k=0 hard-k=1
    show music.words-tap
    show music.words-beats n=8 q=0 hard-n=9 hard-q=1
    show music.words-syllables k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show music.words-stress k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    level medium hard {
      show music.words-fit k=2
    }
    level medium hard {
      show music.words-beats n=11 q=1
    }
    level easy {
      show music.words-beats n=10 q=0
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.words-why r=0 easy-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.words-sing v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "One note for every syllable, and the strongest syllables on the strong beats."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that words have a rhythm of their own, and that setting them to music means one note
    for each syllable with the stressed syllables on the strong beats, so they still sound as they
    are spoken. The wrong ideas to watch for are counting words instead of syllables, and putting a
    weak syllable on beat 1, which makes ba-NA-na sound like BA-na-na. In the working, look for the
    child saying the word aloud and clapping it before choosing. The core counts syllables, finds
    the stressed one, matches a rhythm to a word, taps a line as spoken and works out how many beats
    a line lasts; the two-star asks why a misplaced stress sounds wrong, and the three-star is a
    line of the child's own, sung, which you listen to and tick rather than mark.

    The stresses are as English dictionaries give them (recalled for these common words: AP-ple,
    gui-TAR, po-TA-to, BUT-ter-fly, um-BREL-la, kan-ga-ROO, EL-e-phant, HOL-i-day, to-MA-to,
    ba-NA-na). If the stress is stuck, say the word with each syllable loud in turn and listen for
    the one that sounds right. Off the page, take a nursery rhyme and clap it while saying it, then
    tap the beat: the claps on the taps are the strong syllables. This lesson meets Japan's 2017
    course of study for music, grades 3 and 4, A表現(1)イ: 「曲想と音楽の構造や歌詞の内容との関わりについて気付くこと」 (notice how
    the feel of a song goes with its structure and its words), and England's key stage 2 aim to
    "improvise and compose music for a range of purposes".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the lines are short, the stresses are in two-syllable words, how long a line lasts is worked once in the teacher's pen before lines of all quavers, the rhythm to match is for elephant, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the line sung is one line."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the lines are long, the stresses are in umbrella and kangaroo, the rhythms are for holiday and tomato, the lines end on a held note, and the two lines sung end on a long note."
  }
}
